  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 1250 PM CDT Wed Sep 9 2026\n\nFriday night through Wednesday:\n\nSynopsis: A pair of upper disturbances remain on track to move \nthrough the region Friday night through Saturday & Sunday night \nthrough Monday. Each is expected to bring periods of showers and \nthunderstorms to southern Wisconsin, with precipitation focusing \nalong wings of warm/moist advection leading each system & along \ntrailing cold fronts. Current timing points toward warm advection \nprecip occurring during overnight & predawn periods, with any cold \nfrontal development favored during the following afternoons. \nAfternoon/cold frontal storm coverage will be dependent on \nsufficient thermodynamic recovery in each system. Humid conditions \nwill occur in the interim warm sectors on Saturday/Monday \nafternoons, with air temperatures expected to remain low enough to \npreclude to need for heat headlines. High pressure will slide into \nthe western Great Lakes Tuesday-Wednesday, bringing dry and pleasant \nconditions for the middle of next week.\n\nFriday night through Saturday: First of two primary long term period \nprecip windows opens across southern Wisconsin. Anticipating a warm \nfrontal boundary to work into the area late Friday night/predawn \nSaturday morning, with affiliated shower and thunderstorm chances \nspreading in after midnight. Overnight storm timing should dampen \nwidespread severe weather potential, but will need to monitor for an \nisolated storm with gusty winds given building MUCAPE and increasing \ndeep layer shear. Does appear that there will be an intermediate dry \nperiod ahead of the encroaching cold front Saturday \nmorning/afternoon, with a second chance for showers/storms arriving \nwith the boundary during the later afternoon & evening. Earlier day \nsunshine will be a requisite for afternoon/evening storms, with any \ncombination of overcast skies or lingering rain greatly reducing \npotential. Currently appears that the best chances for recovery will \nbe in south-central and southeast Wisconsin, where the front will \narrive latest in the day. Have thus maintained precip probs \nfrom the NBM in the afternoon update, which at-present appear \nrepresentative of atmospheric expectations. In the event re- \ndevelopment occurs, an isolated stronger storm with gusty winds \nwould be possible. \n\nSunday evening through Monday: A second (and potentially more \ndynamic) system will move through the region, bringing storm chances \nSunday evening/night & once again Monday afternoon/evening. Initial \nglobal guidance points toward greater mass fluxes into this second \nsystem, which would in turn lead to greater instability, deep layer \nshear, and precipitable water values for showers & storms to work \nwith. Will thus need to monitor for some stronger storms with \nlocally heavy rainfall during this portion of the period, with the \nunderstanding that changes in system strength will lead to changes \nin possible hazards. Will start providing more granular details from \nlate week into the weekend as high-res guidance comes into range. \n\nQuigley",
